From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:35:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 40/43] sam: a file that does not exist yet is new, not an
 error, when first read

Under samterm a file is read when a command first needs it, and that
command ran into "can't open" and was cancelled: a/text/ typed on a new
file (sam newfile, the command window) appended nothing, and w wrote an
empty file. The first read (edit's 'I') of a file that does not exist
now warns (no such file) and leaves it new and empty; the command goes
on. Another failure to open it (no permission) is still an error, so
that w cannot write an empty buffer over it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/src/cmd/sam/sam.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sys/src/cmd/sam/sam.c b/sys/src/cmd/sam/sam.c
index 607a9131e..8f4ff6e2c 100644
--- a/sys/src/cmd/sam/sam.c
+++ b/sys/src/cmd/sam/sam.c
@@ -342,6 +342,16 @@ edit(File *f, int cmd)
 	if((io = open(genc, OREAD))<0) {
 		if (curfile && curfile->unread)
 			curfile->unread = FALSE;
+		/*
+		 * the first read of a file that does not exist yet: a new,
+		 * empty file.  Under samterm it comes within the command that
+		 * first needs the file, which an error would cancel (a/text/
+		 * on a new file was lost)
+		 */
+		if(cmd == 'I' && access(genc, AEXIST) < 0){
+			warn_s(Wfile, genc);
+			return;
+		}
 		error_r(Eopen, genc);
 	}
 	p = readio(f, &nulls, empty, TRUE);
